er(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nI found no schema or index definitions in the repo, so the points below assume the usual layout. That layout is `users.id` as the primary key, a plain index on `users.email` (or none), and `orders.user_id` as a foreign key.\n\n**1. `lower(u.email)` stops the email index from being used (main issue)**\n- Wrapping the column in a function makes the predicate non-sargable. A normal index on `users.email` can't be used, so the database scans the whole `users` table and applies `lower()` to every row.\n- Fix, option A: create an expression index, e.g. `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));` in Postgres. The query can then stay as it is.\n- Fix, option B: store emails already normalized to lowercase, or use a case-insensitive type or collation (`citext` in Postgres, a `_ci` collation in MySQL). Then the query can use `u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a plain unique index.\n- Option B also lets you enforce uniqueness case-insensitively.\n\n**2. `orders.user_id` may have no index**\n- After the lookup finds the user, the join has to find that user's orders. Without an index on `orders.user_id`, that is a sequential scan of `orders`.\n- MySQL/InnoDB creates an index for a foreign key automatically. Postgres doesn't, so check there: `CREATE INDEX ON orders (user_id);`\n\n**3. `SELECT *`**\n- It returns every column from both tables, including the `user_id`/`id` columns twice and any wide or text columns. That means more I/O and network transfer, and it blocks index-only scans.\n- Duplicate column names can also break clients that map rows by name.\n- List only the columns you need. If the query only needs a few order columns, a covering index such as `(user_id) INCLUDE (...)` becomes possible.\n\n**4. Unbounded result set**\n- If a user can have many orders, there is no `LIMIT` or ordering. For \"recent orders\", add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n`, with an index on `(user_id, created_at)`.\n\n**Suggested rewrite** (the column names here are placeholders):\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n-- with: CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n--       CREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nIf you tell me which database you use and share the schema, I can tune this further. Running `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)` on a realistic data size would confirm the scan on `users`.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}